{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "ISRM_line",
    "description": "The InMAP ISRM source-receptor model over a LINE (polyline) emission inventory: road centrelines carrying an annual primary-PM2.5 total, allocated to the ISRM source cells by the fraction of each road's length that falls in each cell, contracted with the layer-0 source-receptor matrix, and turned into PM2.5-attributable deaths. It is the third geometry in the family: isrm_point.esm drops a stack's mass into the one cell that contains it, isrm_polygon.esm splits a county's mass across the cells it covers by AREA, and this document splits a road's mass across the cells it crosses by LENGTH. All three share isrm_base.esm - the Lambert conformal projection, the source-receptor contraction term, the ug/m3 scaling and the concentration-response function are the same template bodies - and differ ONLY in how emitted mass reaches a grid cell. Everything downstream of the per-cell emission vector is identical. TWO THINGS THIS ONE DOES THAT THE OTHERS DO NOT. It needs no geometry kernel at all: clipping a straight segment to an axis-aligned rectangle is Liang-Barsky, four divisions of pure algebra, so none of the convexity and vertex-tolerance caveats that attend polygon clipping apply and the allocation is exact to floating point. And its records are SEGMENTS rather than whole roads, with the road recovered inside the model by a group total over the record axis - see metadata.x_esd.one_row_per_segment for why that is the engine's constraint and not a modelling choice. WHY THERE IS NO PLUME RISE HERE: a road has no stack, no exit velocity and no exit temperature, so there is nothing to rise - its mass enters the model at the ground, which is SR emission layer 0, and the document reads only the ISRM_SR_L0 sibling loader.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML"
    ],
    "license": "MIT",
    "created": "2026-08-21T00:00:00Z",
    "tags": [
      "isrm",
      "inmap",
      "air_quality",
      "health",
      "source_receptor",
      "line",
      "roads",
      "mobile_sources",
      "regridding",
      "shapefile"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2019/04/20/sr/",
        "notes": "The InMAP source-receptor tutorial isrm_point.esm reproduces. This document reuses its grid, its SR matrix, its unit conversion and its health function over a different emission geometry; it has no published total of its own to match, because the emission layer it reads is an EXAMPLE this repository builds (data/make_line_layer.py), not an inventory anyone has published a result for."
      },
      {
        "url": "https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/TIGER2020/PRISECROADS/tl_2020_17_prisecroads.zip",
        "notes": "US Census TIGER/Line primary and secondary roads for Illinois, 2020 vintage - the road centrelines data/make_line_layer.py filters to the interstates, thins to the grid's resolution, cuts into segments and attaches an emission column to."
      },
      {
        "url": "https://doi.org/10.1145/357332.357333",
        "notes": "Liang, Y.-D. and Barsky, B. A. (1984), 'A new concept and method for line clipping', ACM TOG 3(1) - the parametric slab clip the `length_share` template states. The document's spelling is the branchless form: the entry and exit parameters are taken with min/max over the slabs rather than by early rejection, because `ifelse` does not guard its arms inside an aggregate in any binding."
      }
    ],
    "x_esd": {
      "emission_column": "The layer's EMIS column is DECLARED, not measured: data/make_line_layer.py sets it to `rate * LEN_KM` short tons per year - a uniform per-kilometre line source at a rate the script's --rate names (1.0 by default). It is read here as PRIMARY PM2.5, the pathway a per-length surrogate is most defensible for and the only one a single undifferentiated tonnage can honestly feed: splitting one number across five pathways would be inventing a speciation profile. The shipped layer (Illinois interstates, 292 roads cut into 1938 segments, 7386.899902 short tons/yr over 7386.9 km) is an EXAMPLE that exercises the geometry. A real on-road inventory carries one column per pollutant, and extending this document to five pathways is five more parameters and five more contractions, exactly as isrm_point.esm has them. NOTE THE REPLICATION: EMIS is the PARENT ROAD's whole annual emission, repeated on every segment row of that road - it is NOT the segment's share. Summing the column would multiply each road's emission by its segment count. `seg_emis` is where the road's total becomes a segment's, by length, and it is the quantity that sums to the inventory.",
      "one_row_per_segment": "EVERY RECORD IS A TWO-VERTEX SEGMENT, and that is forced by the engine rather than chosen. The natural document would read the roads as the shapefile stores them - one record per road, a [record, vertex, xy] geometry array - and range the emission-binning aggregate over (cell, road, vertex), contracting the last two away. That is legal ESM: esm-spec 4.3.1 contracts the SET of index symbols that do not appear in output_idx, and two of them is as ordinary as one. But the projection-pushdown rewrite that makes this model runnable does not recognise it: the binning detector requires the aggregate to declare EXACTLY TWO ranges, one being the output cell set (pushdown_rewrite `_pd_detect_binning`). A three-range aggregate falls out of the pattern, no derived support set is built, no gate reaches the loader, and the whole 33 GB PrimaryPM25 slab is fetched instead of the 757 source rows this layer needs. So the segment is the record, and data/make_line_layer.py does the cutting. WHAT THE DOCUMENT STILL OWNS: every length in the model. `seg_len` is each segment's own planar length, `road_len` recovers its parent road's by summing the segments that share its LINEID, and `length_share` clips inside a cell. The layer hands over coordinates and a parent id, not lengths - its LEN_KM column exists for a human reading the file and is deliberately never read here. That matters: a document that took its denominator from the layer would be testing half of the allocation.",
      "geometry_pipeline": "The shapefile reader delivers `geometry` as [index, vertex, xy] in the file's own CRS - here NAD83 geographic (EPSG:4269), the same lon/lat datum the FF10 point inventory and the county layer use - padded to N_LVERT vertex slots. Every record of this layer is exactly two vertices, so nvert_max = 2 is a width the reader fills exactly and no padding occurs; a layer whose rows were longer would be an error from the reader rather than a silent truncation. Reprojection is the DOCUMENT's job and never the reader's, so line_x / line_y apply the same lcc_forward_x / lcc_forward_y templates isrm_point.esm projects its stack coordinates with. seg_xmin/ymin/xmax/ymax are the extremes of the two PROJECTED endpoints, taken after projection because the Lambert image of a lon/lat box is not a box; for a segment the extremes are exactly the two endpoints, so unlike the polygon document's ring extremes this needs no reduction. There is no ring array on either side of this model: a segment is two points and a cell is four numbers, and the clip reads both directly.",
      "planar_versus_geodesic": "Every length in this model is PLANAR, measured in the Lambert conformal projection the ISRM grid is defined in. The layer's LEN_KM column is GEODESIC, measured on the sphere before projection, and over Illinois the two differ by about half a percent (7350.3 km planar against 7386.9 km geodesic for the shipped layer, the projection's scale factor away from its standard parallels). The document uses the planar one throughout and deliberately does not mix them: the numerator (how much of a segment is inside a cell) and the denominator (how long its road is) have to be in the same metric or the shares do not sum to 1. Mass is conserved because the shares are a ratio of two planar lengths - the metric cancels - not because the planar length happens to be right. Which metric should set the EMISSIONS is a different question and belongs to the inventory, not to this document: the layer answers it with the geodesic length, and the document takes EMIS as given.",
      "report": {
        "description": "What a run reports, in the document's own vocabulary. The three shims emit one cross-language contract record (contract/results_schema.json) and must name no pollutant, no pathway and no observed of their own - this block is where those names live, so the same shim drives all three documents without a table of any of them inside it. `pathways` lists one entry per SR array the model contracts with: `emissions` are the per-source-cell emission observeds that feed it (a list because isrm_point.esm splits a pathway across three SR emission layers; here a ground-level line source reaches only layer 0, so it is one), and `concentration` is the receptor-side result. A key absent here is a block absent from the record: this document declares no `plume`, because it has no plume rise to report.",
        "pathways": [
          {
            "sr_array": "PrimaryPM25",
            "emissions": [
              "E_PM25"
            ],
            "concentration": "conc_PrimaryPM25"
          }
        ],
        "total_pm25": "TotalPM25",
        "deaths": {
          "krewski": "deathsK",
          "lepeule": "deathsL"
        },
        "record_field": "seg_len",
        "record_field_description": "An observed over the RECORD axis, whose length IS the number of emission records the sources discovered. `extent` binds that count to N_REC inside the engine, but no binding exposes the resolved metaparameter on its prepared model, so a runner that must report it reads the length of a field over that axis instead - named here, because which field it is depends on the geometry. For this document a record is a SEGMENT, so the reported count is the segment count and not the road count."
      }
    }
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "N_SRC": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 52411,
      "description": "ISRM source cells - the same grid isrm_point.esm and isrm_polygon.esm emit into."
    },
    "N_RCV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 52411,
      "description": "ISRM receptor cells (== the source grid, same ordered cell set)."
    },
    "N_ALL": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 596444,
      "description": "Every cell of the full InMAP grid: 8 ground layers of N_SRC cells plus 19 coarse layers of 9324, laid out layer-major. The length of W/S/E/N/TotalPop/MortalityRate as the zarr stores them. This document reads only the layer-0 prefix, but it reads the arrays at their real length and slices with a declared `select`, the way the other two do."
    },
    "N_LVERT": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 2,
      "description": "Vertices per emission record, and the reader's `nvert_max`. It is 2 because a record IS a segment (metadata.x_esd.one_row_per_segment): position 1 is the segment's first endpoint and position 2 its second. Unlike the polygon document's N_PVERT this is not the width of a padded ragged array - every row of the layer has exactly two vertices, so the declared width is filled exactly and the reader raises rather than padding or truncating if a rebuilt layer ever disagrees."
    },
    "N_XY": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 2,
      "description": "Components of a planar coordinate: easting at position 1, northing at position 2. The xy axis of the reader's `geometry`, where the two are lon and lat before projection."
    },
    "N_REC": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Emission records - SEGMENTS, not roads. DISCOVERED, not declared: Line_Emis.extent binds it to the number of rows the reader delivers, so a reduced run's `select` truncation re-discovers a smaller value by itself. The default 0 is never used - the loader always supplies it. Truncation is safe here in a way it would not be if the road totals came from the layer: `road_len` is a group total over whatever records survive, so a truncated run allocates the surviving segments' shares of a shorter road and still conserves mass."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "src_cells": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "N_SRC"
    },
    "rcv_cells": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "N_RCV"
    },
    "all_cells": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "N_ALL"
    },
    "emis_records": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "N_REC"
    },
    "line_vertex": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "N_LVERT"
    },
    "xy": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "N_XY"
    }
  },
  "data_sources": {
    "ISRM_Grid": {
      "kind": "static",
      "source": {
        "url_template": "s3://inmap-model/isrm_v1.2.2.zarr/"
      },
      "reference": {
        "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2022/12/15/tutorial/",
        "notes": "InMAP ISRM v1.2.1 zarr store on the inmap-model S3 bucket - the 1-D per-cell arrays: the grid rectangles, population and mortality. A ground-level line source needs no meteorology, so like isrm_polygon.esm and unlike isrm_point.esm this document does not read the eight plume-rise fields."
      },
      "metadata": {
        "esio_format": "zarr",
        "tags": [
          "isrm",
          "inmap",
          "grid",
          "zarr",
          "health"
        ],
        "x_esd": {
          "format": "zarr",
          "zarr_format": 2,
          "consolidated": false,
          "arrays": {
            "TotalPop": {
              "shape": [
                596444
              ],
              "chunks": [
                596444
              ],
              "dtype": "<f8"
            },
            "MortalityRate": {
              "shape": [
                596444
              ],
              "chunks": [
                596444
              ],
              "dtype": "<f8"
            },
            "W": {
              "shape": [
                596444
              ],
              "chunks": [
                596444
              ],
              "dtype": "<f8"
            },
            "S": {
              "shape": [
                596444
              ],
              "chunks": [
                596444
              ],
              "dtype": "<f8"
            },
            "E": {
              "shape": [
                596444
              ],
              "chunks": [
                596444
              ],
              "dtype": "<f8"
            },
            "N": {
              "shape": [
                596444
              ],
              "chunks": [
                596444
              ],
              "dtype": "<f8"
            }
          },
          "cell_layout": "One value per cell of the FULL InMAP grid, laid out LAYER-MAJOR over the model's 27 layers: 8 ground-grid layers of 52411 cells each, then 19 coarse layers of 9324, and 8*52411 + 19*9324 = 596444 = N_ALL. The source and receptor sets are the leading N_SRC / N_RCV cells - model layer 0 - which is what the `select` ranges below take.",
          "note": "`format` and the pinned .zarray params are not expressible in the 0.8.0 source schema; the runner sets format at provider creation. Everything here is a single-chunk 1-D array read in full."
        }
      }
    },
    "ISRM_SR_L0": {
      "kind": "static",
      "source": {
        "url_template": "s3://inmap-model/isrm_v1.2.2.zarr/"
      },
      "reference": {
        "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2022/12/15/tutorial/",
        "notes": "InMAP ISRM v1.2.1 zarr store, emission layer 0 - the only emission layer a ground-level line source reaches."
      },
      "metadata": {
        "esio_format": "zarr",
        "tags": [
          "isrm",
          "source_receptor",
          "air_quality",
          "zarr",
          "emis_layer_0"
        ],
        "x_esd": {
          "format": "zarr",
          "zarr_format": 2,
          "consolidated": false,
          "sr_dim_order": [
            "emisLayer",
            "source",
            "receptor"
          ],
          "arrays": {
            "PrimaryPM25": {
              "shape": [
                3,
                52411,
                52411
              ],
              "chunks": [
                1,
                5,
                52411
              ],
              "dtype": "<f4",
              "compressor": "blosc:lz4:clevel5:shuffle1",
              "fill": 0.0,
              "order": "C"
            }
          },
          "gated_select": {
            "axes": [
              {
                "fixed": [
                  0
                ]
              },
              {
                "gated_by": "emis_src_cells"
              },
              "all"
            ],
            "applies_to": [
              "PrimaryPM25"
            ]
          },
          "note": "Per-axis selection over the native SR dims [emisLayer, source, receptor] = [3, 52411, 52411]. `{\"fixed\":[0]}` takes emission layer 0 and DROPS the axis; `{\"gated_by\":\"emis_src_cells\"}` takes the DERIVED support set the pushdown rewrite invents from this document's own spatial gate, in its canonical member order; `\"all\"` keeps the receptor axis. The support set here is every cell whose rectangle a road SEGMENT's envelope overlaps, and the rewrite works that out from the gate - the difference between the three documents never leaves the model. Roads are thin, so this is the smallest gate of the three: 757 of 52411 source rows for the shipped Illinois layer, against 2246 for the county layer."
        }
      }
    },
    "Line_Emis": {
      "kind": "static",
      "source": {
        "url_template": "file://data/line_emissions_17.zip"
      },
      "reference": {
        "notes": "The example line-emission layer this repository BUILDS rather than fetches: `python data/make_line_layer.py` filters the Census TIGER road centrelines to one state's interstates, thins each road by Douglas-Peucker at the grid's own resolution, cuts it into two-vertex segments, attaches EMIS = rate * LEN_KM short tons per year, and writes the four-file shapefile set as one zip (the zip is the fetchable form because the content-addressed cache holds one blob per URL). There is no upstream copy of the RESULT to point at, because the result is generated, so the url is a `file:` url relative to this checkout - run the builder before running the model. A shim resolves it to an absolute path by basename, the same locality override it uses to read the FF10 point inventory from disk instead of gaftp.epa.gov."
      },
      "metadata": {
        "esio_format": "shapefile",
        "tags": [
          "shapefile",
          "polylines",
          "roads",
          "line_sources",
          "emissions"
        ],
        "x_esd": {
          "format": "shapefile",
          "container": "zip",
          "crs": "EPSG:4269",
          "note": "The reader is READER-ONLY: it decodes vertices and .dbf columns and declares the file's CRS, and it reprojects nothing. `member` names the .shp inside the zip (the .dbf/.shx/.prj sidecars are the same stem) and never enters the cache key; `nvert_max` is N_LVERT = 2, the declared vertex width. One row per shapefile PART - which for this layer is one row per segment, because the builder writes each segment as its own single-part record."
        }
      },
      "reader_options": {
        "member": "emis_lines.shp",
        "nvert_max": 2
      },
      "extent": {
        "metaparameter": "N_REC"
      }
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "ISRM": {
      "system_kind": "nonlinear",
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "./isrm_base.esm"
        }
      ],
      "reference": {
        "url": "https://inmap.run/blog/2019/04/20/sr/",
        "notes": "Same grid, same SR matrix, same unit conversion and same health function as isrm_point.esm and isrm_polygon.esm; a third emission geometry."
      },
      "variables": {
        "W": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "all_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Cell west edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.W (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "W"
            }
          }
        },
        "S": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "all_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Cell south edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.S (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "S"
            }
          }
        },
        "E": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "all_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Cell east edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.E (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "E"
            }
          }
        },
        "N": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "all_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Cell north edge (LCC m); from ISRM_Grid.N (full N_ALL, the layer-0 prefix used).",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "N"
            }
          }
        },
        "TotalPop": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "all_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Population per cell; from ISRM_Grid.TotalPop (read on the rcv range = the [0:N_RCV] prefix slice).",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "TotalPop"
            }
          }
        },
        "MortalityRate": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "all_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Baseline mortality per 100k; from ISRM_Grid.MortalityRate (prefix [0:N_RCV]).",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "MortalityRate"
            }
          }
        },
        "src_W": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "src_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Source-cell rectangle W-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid W. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the rectangle `length_share` clips each segment against - there is no cell ring in this document, because a straight segment clips against four numbers and never needs the cell turned into a polygon.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "W",
              "select": {
                "axes": [
                  {
                    "range": {
                      "start": 0,
                      "stop": "N_SRC"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "src_S": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "src_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Source-cell rectangle S-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid S. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the rectangle `length_share` clips each segment against - there is no cell ring in this document, because a straight segment clips against four numbers and never needs the cell turned into a polygon.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "S",
              "select": {
                "axes": [
                  {
                    "range": {
                      "start": 0,
                      "stop": "N_SRC"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "src_E": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "src_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Source-cell rectangle E-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid E. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the rectangle `length_share` clips each segment against - there is no cell ring in this document, because a straight segment clips against four numbers and never needs the cell turned into a polygon.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "E",
              "select": {
                "axes": [
                  {
                    "range": {
                      "start": 0,
                      "stop": "N_SRC"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "src_N": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "src_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Source-cell rectangle N-edge (projected LCC metres), src_cells axis: the declared [0:N_SRC] prefix of the full-grid N. These four are the cell side of the broad-phase gate AND the rectangle `length_share` clips each segment against - there is no cell ring in this document, because a straight segment clips against four numbers and never needs the cell turned into a polygon.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_Grid",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "N",
              "select": {
                "axes": [
                  {
                    "range": {
                      "start": 0,
                      "stop": "N_SRC"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "SR_PrimaryPM25_L0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "ug/m^3/(kg/yr)",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "src_cells",
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Source-receptor matrix for the primary PM2.5 pathway from emission layer 0. Rows are the emission-bearing source cells the rewrite derives (emis_src_cells) - for this document, every cell a road segment's envelope overlaps - and columns the N_RCV receptors. Wired from ISRM_SR_L0.PrimaryPM25; the loader's own gated_select fixes the emission-layer axis, so this stays rank-2.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "ISRM_SR_L0",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "PrimaryPM25"
            }
          }
        },
        "line_lonlat": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "degree",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "emis_records",
            "line_vertex",
            "xy"
          ],
          "description": "Every segment's two endpoints, as the shapefile stores them: [record, vertex, component] in the file's own CRS (EPSG:4269 lon/lat). Component 1 is longitude and component 2 is latitude; vertex 1 is where the segment starts and vertex 2 where it ends, and which is which does not matter to anything downstream - every quantity built from them is symmetric in the two. From Line_Emis.geometry, and the only geometry input.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "Line_Emis",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "geometry"
            }
          }
        },
        "line_emis": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "short_ton/yr",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "The PARENT ROAD's annual primary PM2.5 emission in short tons per year, from Line_Emis.EMIS - replicated onto every segment row of that road, the way the shapefile reader replicates a record's attributes across its parts. It is NOT this segment's emission: summing this column would count each road once per segment. `seg_emis` is the segment's share of it, by length. See metadata.x_esd.emission_column - the column is an example quantity derived from road length, and reading it as primary PM2.5 is this document's declaration.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "Line_Emis",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "EMIS"
            }
          }
        },
        "line_key": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "The parent road's integer identifier, from Line_Emis.LINEID: the segments of one road share it, and nothing else does. It is the group key `road_len` sums over. Read as a number rather than as text because a group key has to be compared, and the .dbf numeric column arrives as a whole-numbered float64 that compares exactly - see the `group_total_term` template note. The layer's SEGID column, which orders the segments along their road, is deliberately not read: the allocation is a sum over a group and does not care in which order the road was traversed.",
          "update": {
            "kind": "data",
            "source": "Line_Emis",
            "from": {
              "file_variable": "LINEID"
            }
          }
        },
        "fact": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 28766.639396245562,
          "description": "short_ton/year emission-unit -> ug/m3 scaling (applied once to the summed concentration). Exactly InMAP's emisConversionFactor, 907184740000 / (3600*8760)."
        },
        "pop_scale": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1.0465819687408728,
          "description": "Population 2010->2016 rescale."
        },
        "mort_scale": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1.025229357798165,
          "description": "Mortality-rate 2005->2016 rescale."
        },
        "rr_K": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1.06,
          "description": "Krewski relative risk per 10 ug/m3 PM2.5."
        },
        "rr_L": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1.14,
          "description": "Lepeule relative risk per 10 ug/m3 PM2.5."
        },
        "lat_1": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "degree",
          "default": 33.0,
          "description": "LCC standard parallel 1 (NEI2016)."
        },
        "lat_2": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "degree",
          "default": 45.0,
          "description": "LCC standard parallel 2 (NEI2016)."
        },
        "lat_0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "degree",
          "default": 40.0,
          "description": "LCC origin latitude (NEI2016)."
        },
        "lon_0": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "degree",
          "default": -97.0,
          "description": "LCC central meridian (NEI2016)."
        },
        "lcc_R": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 6370997.0,
          "description": "LCC sphere radius (NEI2016). (Named lcc_R, not R, to avoid the reserved physical-constant name R = ideal gas constant.)"
        },
        "lcc_d2r": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "default": 0.017453292519943295,
          "description": "degrees->radians (pi/180) for the LCC projection."
        },
        "lcc_qp": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "default": 0.7853981633974483,
          "description": "pi/4 quarter-turn offset for the LCC tan half-angle."
        },
        "clip_eps": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "default": 1e-09,
          "description": "The floor `length_share` puts under the magnitude of a segment's x- and y-extent before dividing by it, so that a segment exactly parallel to an axis does not divide by zero. One nanometre: far below any distance this grid or this inventory can resolve, and far enough below a cell edge (1 km at the finest) that the two slab parameters it produces are of order 1e12 and the min/max reduce them to 'no constraint' or 'empty' with no rounding in between. It is a numerical guard, not a tolerance - it never merges two distinct points and never changes an answer that was already defined."
        },
        "line_x": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records",
            "line_vertex"
          ],
          "description": "Lambert-conformal-conic forward-x of every segment endpoint, in the InMAP grid's own projected metres - the same lcc_forward_x template isrm_point.esm projects its emission points and isrm_polygon.esm its ring vertices with. A pure MAP over (record, vertex)."
        },
        "line_y": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records",
            "line_vertex"
          ],
          "description": "Lambert-conformal-conic forward-y of every segment endpoint. See line_x."
        },
        "seg_xmin": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "The western extreme of the PROJECTED segment - the record side of the broad-phase gate. A segment's extremes are its two endpoints, so this is a min of two numbers rather than a reduction over a vertex axis as the polygon document's ring extremes are. Taken AFTER projection, because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle and a gate built on the file's stored bounding box would not be conservative - and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently."
        },
        "seg_xmax": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "The eastern extreme of the PROJECTED segment - the record side of the broad-phase gate. A segment's extremes are its two endpoints, so this is a max of two numbers rather than a reduction over a vertex axis as the polygon document's ring extremes are. Taken AFTER projection, because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle and a gate built on the file's stored bounding box would not be conservative - and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently."
        },
        "seg_ymin": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "The southern extreme of the PROJECTED segment - the record side of the broad-phase gate. A segment's extremes are its two endpoints, so this is a min of two numbers rather than a reduction over a vertex axis as the polygon document's ring extremes are. Taken AFTER projection, because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle and a gate built on the file's stored bounding box would not be conservative - and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently."
        },
        "seg_ymax": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "The northern extreme of the PROJECTED segment - the record side of the broad-phase gate. A segment's extremes are its two endpoints, so this is a max of two numbers rather than a reduction over a vertex axis as the polygon document's ring extremes are. Taken AFTER projection, because the Lambert image of a lon/lat rectangle is not a rectangle and a gate built on the file's stored bounding box would not be conservative - and a gate that is not conservative drops real overlaps silently."
        },
        "seg_len": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "Each segment's own length in projected metres. PLANAR, in the grid's coordinates, and deliberately not the layer's geodesic LEN_KM - see metadata.x_esd.planar_versus_geodesic. It appears twice in the allocation, as the numerator of the segment's share of its road and (through `length_share`, which normalises by it implicitly) as the length the clip is a fraction of; both uses are in the same metric, which is what makes the shares sum to 1. A zero-length segment would make `seg_emis` divide 0 by 0, so the builder drops one before it reaches the layer."
        },
        "road_len": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "The total planar length of the ROAD this segment belongs to, replicated onto each of its segments: the sum of seg_len over every record sharing this one's line_key. This is the denominator that turns a segment's length into its share of its parent road, and the document computes it rather than reading the layer's LEN_KM for two reasons - LEN_KM is geodesic where this must be planar, and a denominator taken from the input would leave the half of the allocation that assembles the road untested. It is a SELF-JOIN over the record axis: both index symbols range over emis_records, the shape of the format's canonical prefix-scan spelling, so it costs N_REC^2 term evaluations (1938^2 for the shipped layer, milliseconds at build time). The alternative - a separate road axis sized by a metaparameter - would have to be kept in step with the layer by hand and would give wrong totals whenever a reduced run truncated the records. This spelling stays correct under truncation: the groups shrink with the record set and the shares still sum to 1 over what survives. The contracted symbol is `q`, and it deliberately is not `t`: see the `group_total_term` template note and BUGS.md - a contracted index symbol named `t` is read as the TIME variable by the Python binding's build-time hoist, which silently makes this observed, and everything downstream of it, unreadable."
        },
        "seg_emis": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "short_ton/yr",
          "shape": [
            "emis_records"
          ],
          "description": "This SEGMENT's annual primary PM2.5 emission: its parent road's total times the fraction of the road's length that this segment is, line_emis[r] * seg_len[r] / road_len[r]. This is the uniform-along-the-road assumption stated explicitly - a road emits in proportion to its length, so a segment gets its length's worth - and it is the step that undoes the replication in the EMIS column. Summed over a road's segments it is the road's emission exactly, and summed over the layer it is the inventory: 7386.899902 short tons/yr for the shipped Illinois layer, which is what the cell totals must add back up to."
        },
        "E_PM25": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "short_ton/yr",
          "shape": [
            "src_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Primary PM2.5 emissions binned into each source cell: the sum over segments r of that segment's emission times the fraction of its length that lies in cell c. THIS IS THE ONE EQUATION THAT DIFFERS FROM THE SIBLINGS - isrm_point.esm weights by a 0/1 containment of a point, isrm_polygon.esm by a continuous AREA share, and this document by a continuous LENGTH share; everything after it is the same model. The gate ifelse MUST stay the FIRST ifelse in this body: the rewrite reads it to derive the support set and the SR gate, and a body it cannot recognise does not fail, it silently drops out of `applies_to` and the whole 33 GB PrimaryPM25 slab is fetched ungated instead of 757 of its 52411 source rows. `length_share` contains ifelses of its own - the safe divisors - and they are safely later in the walk because the gate is the first factor of the product and the recogniser takes the first ifelse in pre-order. Mass is conserved to floating point for a road inside the grid: the shares of a segment over the cells it crosses sum to exactly 1, with no geometry kernel and so no vertex-merging tolerance to leak through - which is the one place this document is cleaner than isrm_polygon.esm, where the same statement holds only to 6.1e-7. A road running off the edge of the domain loses exactly the length that runs off."
        },
        "conc_PrimaryPM25": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "ug/m^3",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Per-receptor concentration response from the primary PM2.5 pathway: the layer-0 SR matrix contracted with the per-cell emissions. One contraction rather than isrm_point.esm's three, because a ground-level line source reaches only emission layer 0."
        },
        "pm_PrimaryPM25": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "ug/m^3",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Reported PrimaryPM25 PM2.5 component at each receptor: fact*conc_PrimaryPM25."
        },
        "TotalPM25": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "ug/m^3",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Total PM2.5 concentration at each receptor. The example layer feeds one pathway, so this is fact*conc_PrimaryPM25 and equals pm_PrimaryPM25; it is written separately because it is the quantity the health function consumes, and a five-pathway version of this document adds terms here and nowhere else."
        },
        "deathsK": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Attributable deaths at each receptor (Krewski RR 1.06): (exp(log(rr)/10*TotalPM25)-1)*TotalPop*pop_scale*MortalityRate/100000*mort_scale. TotalPop/MortalityRate indexed on the rcv range = the [0:N_RCV] prefix slice."
        },
        "deathsL": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Attributable deaths at each receptor (Lepeule RR 1.14): (exp(log(rr)/10*TotalPM25)-1)*TotalPop*pop_scale*MortalityRate/100000*mort_scale. TotalPop/MortalityRate indexed on the rcv range = the [0:N_RCV] prefix slice."
        },
        "rcv_W": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Receptor cell rectangle west edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid W. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
        },
        "rcv_S": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Receptor cell rectangle south edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid S. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
        },
        "rcv_E": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Receptor cell rectangle east edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid E. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
        },
        "rcv_N": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Receptor cell rectangle north edge in LCC metres: the [0:N_RCV] prefix of the full-grid N. Written so a run's output store carries the geometry its fields are over."
        },
        "rcv_cx": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Receptor cell-centroid easting in LCC metres."
        },
        "rcv_cy": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "rcv_cells"
          ],
          "description": "Receptor cell-centroid northing in LCC metres."
        }
      },
      "equations": [
        {
          "lhs": "line_x",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "output_idx": [
              "r",
              "v"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "r": {
                "from": "emis_records"
              },
              "v": {
                "from": "line_vertex"
              }
